          <p>
            The whey isolate is the brand's best seller on its own site, and the label explains why. Per the label at the time of writing, a 30 g serving has 25 g of protein, 110 calories, 0 g of fat and 0 g of sugar, and the only additive is sunflower lecithin, which helps the powder wet and disperse. If you want a fast-digesting post-workout shake rather than a slow casein, this is the BulkSupplements powder to buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The hydrolyzed whey isolate is the one that puzzles us. Its published nutrition panel matches the standard isolate's almost exactly: 25 g of protein, 110 calories, 16 mg of cholesterol, 55 mg of sodium, and the same amino acid profile down to 9.2 g of leucine per 100 g. Its ingredient line on the label image also reads whey protein isolate and sunflower lecithin, without the word hydrolyzed. That does not prove the powders are the same, since hydrolysis does not change the protein count, but BulkSupplements does not publish a degree of hydrolysis, and nothing on the label justifies paying about 75 percent more per gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            The organic pea protein carries the USDA Organic seal and lists no allergens. It gives 21 g of protein, 120 calories and 2.3 g of fat per 30 g serving, with 320 mg of sodium and 7 mg of iron, 40 percent of the Daily Value. The soy isolate gives 25 g of protein in just 90 calories, with 3 g of fiber but 380 mg of sodium. Both are useful for anyone avoiding dairy; our <a href="/best-pea-protein-powder/">best pea protein powder</a> and <a href="/best-soy-protein-powder/">best soy protein powder</a> guides compare them with other brands.
          </p>
