        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is BulkSupplements Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, according to the brand: BulkSupplements says each supplement is tested by a third-party laboratory before distribution. The exact wording on its About page is that each supplement is tested by a 3rd party laboratory before distribution, and that it uses reputable and vetted US-based independent labs. Its product pages also carry a Trusted Quality graphic with three badges: Third Party Tested, Manufactured to cGMP Standards and Quality Inspected.
          </p>

          <p>
            BulkSupplements also says its products are made in an NSF Certified and FDA-registered cGMP facility in Henderson, Nevada, run by Hard Eight Nutrition LLC, the company behind the brand. Be careful with that phrase. An NSF-certified facility means the manufacturing site has passed a good manufacturing practice audit. It is not the same as NSF Certified for Sport, which tests finished products for banned substances and is listed product by product in NSF's public database.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of the BulkSupplements proteins we checked claim NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice, and we found no such certification on the brand's product pages. The brand also does not name the labs it uses or publish the results of its testing on the product pages. So the honest summary is: the brand says it tests, the testing is not independently verifiable from public information, and there is no banned-substance certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most people that is fine. A single-ingredient casein or whey isolate has very little room for the kinds of contamination that sport certification targets, such as stimulants or anabolic agents slipped into proprietary blends. For drug-tested athletes, though, the rule is simple: only use products listed in a certifier's database. If that is you, look at the certified options in our <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">best casein protein powder</a> roundup instead.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHAT THE CLAIM MEANS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Third-party tested is not the same as third-party certified</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">BulkSupplements says an outside lab tests each product before it ships. Certification programs such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport go further: they test for banned substances, audit the factory, and publish a searchable list. BulkSupplements claims the first, not the second.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="heavy-metals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Does BulkSupplements Protein Contain Lead or Heavy Metals?
          </h2>

          <p>
            BulkSupplements does not publish heavy-metal results on its protein product pages, but the casein, organic pea and soy isolate pages carry a California Prop 65 warning that the product can expose you to lead. When we checked, the standard whey isolate and hydrolyzed whey isolate pages did not show that warning, although one buyer review of the hydrolyzed isolate mentions a recent lead warning, so check the bag you receive.
          </p>

          <p>
            A Prop 65 warning is not proof that a product is unsafe. California's threshold for lead under Prop 65 is very low, 0.5 micrograms a day for reproductive harm, and many foods and plant proteins trip it because lead occurs naturally in soil. Pea and other plant proteins tend to carry more heavy metals than dairy proteins for exactly that reason. Still, a warning tells you a company decided it could not rule out exceeding that level, and it is fair to weigh that against a brand that publishes test results.
          </p>

          <p>
            What about certificates of analysis? We found no batch COAs posted on the BulkSupplements product pages. One verified buyer review on the soy isolate page says the company provided a COA on request and that it covered heavy metals and other contaminants. That matches how many ingredient suppliers work: the paperwork exists but you have to ask. If heavy metals are your main concern, email customer service with the lot number printed on your bag before you open it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are pregnant, breastfeeding or buying for a child, the lead warning is a good reason to talk to a doctor or registered dietitian first and to favor a product that publishes per-batch heavy-metal results. For everyone else, using one serving a day rather than three and rotating between protein sources keeps any exposure from a single product small.
          </p>
        </div>

