        <div id="taste-mixing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Do Buyers Say About BulkSupplements Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers say BulkSupplements casein tastes bland and mildly milky, and that it mixes acceptably but not perfectly in water. One reviewer on the brand's site described it as tasting like mild cottage cheese with a little sour cream, and easy to flavor. Another called it bland like advertised but doable, with some foam left at the bottom and a slightly grainy texture.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most common complaint about the casein is texture. The harshest review we read said it would not dissolve, leaving thick glue at the bottom of the cup and froth on top. That is typical of unflavored casein, which thickens as it hydrates and has no gums or lecithin to help it disperse. Buyers who are happy with it tend to use a blender, mix it into Greek yogurt or overnight oats, or let it sit for a few minutes to make a pudding.
          </p>

          <p>
            The whey isolate gets the best mixing feedback in the range. Buyers on the brand's site say it dissolves well, blends well and mixes with anything, and several use it in smoothies, oatmeal and even soups. That fits the label: the sunflower lecithin in the whey isolates is there precisely to help the powder wet. A buyer of the hydrolyzed isolate said it mixes well in a shaker bottle and does not cause bloating.
          </p>

          <p>
            The plant proteins get more mixed reviews on taste. Buyers of the organic pea protein say it works and mixes okay but that the taste is not great, which is normal for an unflavored pea powder. Soy isolate buyers range from absolutely tasteless and blends in well to a little too bitter. If you plan to drink any of these in plain water, add cocoa powder, instant coffee, a spoon of nut butter or a flavored milk; our <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">best unflavored protein powder</a> guide has more ideas.
          </p>

          <p>
            One complaint crosses every product: the packaging. Several buyers say the zip closure on the resealable bags does not hold, so plan to tip the powder into an airtight tub. Larger bags also ship in boxes that can arrive taped and torn, according to one buyer of the 44 lb whey isolate, though the inner bag was intact.
          </p>
        </div>

