        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy BulkSupplements, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            BulkSupplements suits cost-focused buyers who want one-ingredient protein to cook with or blend into other foods. If you make overnight oats, protein pudding, pancakes or smoothies, a plain casein or whey isolate disappears into the recipe and costs less per gram than almost any flavored tub. It also suits people avoiding sucralose, stevia and other sweeteners, since nothing here is sweetened.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is a good fit for people who want to mix their own blends too. You could combine the casein and whey isolate for a fast-and-slow shake, or pair pea with a little soy to raise the leucine content of a plant shake, and the bulk sizes make that cheap. Older adults who struggle to eat enough protein often use unflavored powder this way, stirred into soups or yogurt; for a ready-made senior formula, see today's <a href="/swanson-senior-muscle-retention-protein-review/">Swanson Senior Muscle Retention protein review</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip BulkSupplements if you are a drug-tested athlete, because none of these powders carry a banned-substance certification. Skip it if you want a shake that tastes good in water with no effort, because these are flavorless and the casein is gritty in a shaker. And skip the casein, pea and soy if a Prop 65 lead warning is a deal-breaker, or ask for the COA first. If you shop in person, our <a href="/sprouts-protein-powder/">Sprouts protein powder</a> guide covers a store that stocks several single-ingredient brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, protein powder is food, not medicine. Anyone with kidney disease, a milk or soy allergy, or a medical condition that limits protein should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding 25 g servings to their day. BulkSupplements' own label says the same for pregnant or nursing women and anyone taking medication.
          </p>
