          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Scooping by volume instead of weighing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                BulkSupplements lists the serving as 4 tablespoons, about 30 g, and ships most bags without a scoop. Fluffy casein and dense soy pack very differently, so 4 tablespoons can be 25 g one day and 35 g the next. Weigh the first few servings on a kitchen scale and note how many level spoonfuls that takes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying extra for the hydrolyzed isolate by default</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The hydrolyzed whey isolate costs about $3.06 per 25 g on Amazon against about $1.73 for the plain isolate, yet both labels show 25 g of protein, 110 calories and the same amino acid profile. Unless plain isolate truly upsets your stomach, the regular whey isolate is the better buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shaking casein in plain water and expecting a smooth drink</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Unflavored casein has no gums or lecithin, so it foams and clumps in a shaker. Buyers who like it blend it, stir it into yogurt or oats, or let it thicken into pudding. Use a blender or a milk base, add the powder after the liquid, and give it a minute to hydrate.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading third-party tested as sport certified</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                BulkSupplements says an outside lab tests each product, but it holds no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing for its proteins. If you are subject to drug testing, that difference matters. Search the certifier's own database by product name before you buy any powder for competition use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 20 kg bag with nowhere to store it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 20 kg casein brings the cost down to about $0.72 per 25 g, but 44 lb of powder in a bag with an unreliable zip needs airtight containers and a cool, dry spot. Moisture causes clumping and off flavors long before the best-by date. Start with 1 kg or 5 kg if unsure.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

